              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 57,
                "start": 57
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Murtagh, congress may provide a removal mechanism to federal court, but the State cannot impose its own policy judgment over congressional decisions regarding federal claims."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Murtagh",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-818279ac54c8-0003-0002"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 58,
                "start": 58
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Murtagh, the petitioner cannot bring his Section\u202f1983 claim in any state court because New York law bars suits against employees and the State is not a person under Supreme Court precedent (Will v. Michigan)."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Ms. Underwood",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0006"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 25,
                "start": 25
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Ms. Underwood, the Federal Constitution permits state courts to hear federal claims but does not require them to do so, provided the state does not discriminate against federal claims."
        }
      ],
